Subject: Variance explained with glmer using a binomial DV
Message-ID: <CAKpA2X9w=0-Jgu4byaJ7WdA8z=BBh0JZ5kSo70hvKhAaCKno+Q@mail.gmail.com>

Hi Ling-R,

I'm comparing two methods for quantifying lexical competition by creating
models that contain one method each and another nested model that contains
both methods. I then used the anova function to compare the model with both
methods to the models with each method individually.

The anovas tell me whether the nested model provides a better fit than the
models with only one method, but I've also been asked by a reviewer to
include information about the additional variance explained by the nested
model. I've found Baayen's recommendation (chapter 7 in his textbook) to
use the squared correlation of the fitted and observed values as a model R^2.
However, my DV is word identification accuracy, a binomial variable.
Therefore, a correlation isn't appropriate.

What other methods are appropriate for contextualizing the improvement in
fit of the nested model for a binomial DV?

Best,
Julia

