Message-ID: <BA62B582-A42A-4880-A475-5B5878A8DBA4@gmail.com>

There's also a simpler reason why the grand mean doesn't equal the intercept, even without random effects.

Say you have one predictor with levels A and B, that will always come out as +X and -X in sum contrasts with the intercept bang in the middle.

But if A has more observations than B or vice versa, the grand mean will not be equal to the intercept. It will be skewed by the token numbers, in a way that the intercept (and other coefficients) never are.

Is it not so?
Dan

